.TH FOLD 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
fold \- filter for folding lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fold
.RB [ \-bs ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fold
copies each
.I file
to standard output, breaking the lines of its input so that no output
line exceeds
.I width
column positions (default 80).
.PP
Three characters affect the column position that is tracked while
folding, unless
.B \-b
is given:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fI<tab>\fR advances the column position to the next tab stop, fixed
every 8 columns.
.IP \(bu 2
\fI<backspace>\fR decrements the column position by one; it never
becomes negative.
.IP \(bu 2
\fI<carriage\-return>\fR resets the column position to zero.
.RE
.PP
A single unit (byte, or decoded character) that is by itself wider
than
.I width
is still emitted, alone, on its own output line, rather than being
split or dropped.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Count
.I width
in bytes rather than column positions. Every byte, including
\fI<tab>\fR, \fI<backspace>\fR, and \fI<carriage\-return>\fR, counts
as exactly one; those three characters lose their special
column\-tracking meaning entirely in this mode.
.TP
.B \-s
If a segment of a line contains a \fI<blank>\fR (space or tab) within
the first
.I width
column positions (or bytes, with
.BR \-b ),
break the line after the last such \fI<blank>\fR instead of exactly
at
.IR width .
If there is no qualifying \fI<blank>\fR,
.B \-s
has no effect for that output segment.
.TP
.BI \-w " width"
Fold lines at
.I width
column positions (or bytes, with
.BR \-b )
instead of the default of 80.
.I width
must be a positive decimal integer.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used. If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
Without
.BR \-b ,
each input character is decoded as UTF\-8 and counted as exactly one
column, regardless of that character's actual display width; wide
characters (for example, many CJK characters that occupy two display
cells) are not given any special treatment.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be opened or read; processing continues with the
next operand.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an invalid option or an invalid
.I width
argument.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cut (1),
.BR pr (1),
.BR wc (1)
